#!/usr/bin/env bash
# lib/steady-state.sh — PRODUCT steady-state hook (worked example).
#
# The chaos kit ships health-only steady state. To make the gate judge your
# actual business flows, COPY this file to lib/steady-state.sh (same
# directory), edit it to run YOUR probes, and never rename the two hook
# functions. The file is sourced once at kit startup — it must only DEFINE
# functions, no top-level side effects.
#
# Hook contract (both functions: exit 0 = pass, non-zero = fail):
#
#   flow_canary LABEL
#       One real, self-contained business-flow probe (a guest journey, a
#       checkout, whatever your product cannot live without). It runs:
#         - foreground, as the baseline/recovery verdict leg (quick output
#           is swallowed; tee to your own log file if you need detail)
#         - background, while postgres/service-kill faults land midwrite —
#           so it must be safe to run concurrently with itself and must
#           clean up its own probe footprint.
#       Keep it idempotent and reasonably fast (< a few minutes).
#
#   tenancy_sweep
#       Tenancy-fence probes for the recovery leg of
#       tenancy-fence-under-fault (--full). Assert the fence for EVERY
#       tenant, not just the resident one, once the control plane is back.
#
# Without this hook installed, canary/sweep legs record as SKIP — the kit
# still runs, just shallower.

# ─── Example shape (adapt to your product) ────────────────────────────────
# The proven shape: a committed probe script in the app repo does the real
# work; the hook just runs it with the right env. Prefer that shape — the
# chaos kit should never own product probe logic.

